== a father who wants his grown daughter found
tier: everyday
sphere: prot
ladder: pillar
at: employer
rate: 0.5
share: .0004
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-75
calls: UB
holds: private investigator
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: week
roles: parent, colleague
earth: a father offers to pay well if you find his grown daughter, who moved away three years ago and has not called since
times: Most private investigators, a few times in their working years; requests to trace an adult who left by choice are a known trouble of tracing work (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: A man in a good coat offers {N} a fat fee to find his daughter. She is twenty-six, moved away three years ago and has not called since. He will not say why.
scene W: An adult may choose who knows where she lives. Nothing gives {N} the right to undo that.
scene U: Finding her would take a week: old friends, a work profile online, a change of address.
scene B: The fee would cover the office rent until spring, and he hints at more work from his firm.
scene R: {N} does not trust the way he will not meet {Ns} eyes.
scene G: {N} thinks of {Ns} own family, and the years {N} went without calling {parent}.
- W | take the case only if she is asked first whether she wants to be found | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: prot.W | chance: 75
- U | ask him the questions he is avoiding before you agree to anything | act: ask him the questions he is avoiding before they agree to anything | v: security, universalism | sphere: prot.U | chance: 75
- B | take the fee, find the address, and ask no more questions | v: achievement, power | closed: approval: tracing an adult who chose to leave, without her say; backfire: she learns who found her | sphere: prot.B | chance: 75
- R | turn him down flat, and show him the door | v: self-direction | mark: turned down a chance | sphere: prot.R | chance: 75
- G | offer to carry a letter from him, if she can be found, and let her answer | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: prot.G | chance: 75
- W>R | tell him straight that the law protects her choice, and you will not get round it | act: tell him straight that the law protects her choice, and they will not get round it | v: universalism, self-direction | sphere: prot.W | chance: 65
- U>G | trace her quietly through old friends, and ask them to say her father is looking | v: benevolence, achievement | sphere: prot.U | chance: 65
- B>W | write a contract that hands nothing over without her signed consent | v: security, conformity | sphere: prot.B | chance: 65
- R>U | watch him for a day before deciding, to see what kind of man he is | v: stimulation, security | sphere: prot.R | chance: 65
- G>B | send him to a friend who runs a family mediation service, for a small finder's fee | v: tradition, power | sphere: prot.G | chance: 65
works: The daughter reads her father's letter, and a month later writes back a few lines.
works: The man thanks {N} for the honest answer, and his firm sends work in the spring.
fails: He storms out, and a bad review of {Ns} office appears online that night.
fails: The daughter hears that someone was asking about her, and changes her number again.

